=== entry a50d8be3e67e
kind: translation
rules: delegate, cursor-only, team-kit, verify-surface, invoke
intent: Every agent this run starts gets `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.` as the first line of its `delegate_task` brief, so worker.md's operator rules reach it. Upstream's "whether a Task subagent or a Project worker" is dropped: a Project worker is a cloud subagent of a Cursor Project coordinator (upstream's overnight guide page), and here every agent is a `delegate_task` child (no rule covers thread launches, so they are not named). "Only load from a slash command" becomes "only load when the user starts them", so it holds for `/` and `$`. For a `poteto-agent`, the rest of the brief also carries the two paths the Subagents section names. `/deslop` is optional and named in bold, UI and CLI shipping use the project's verification skill or T3's preview tools and the shell, and the clause warning off Cursor's built-in babysit skill is dropped. Every other trigger stays verbatim.
--- upstream
- First message → restate the user's goal and the smallest change that meets it, then proceed. Open each subagent brief, PR `## Why`, and final report with the goal.
- Starting any agent, whether a Task subagent or a Project worker → the first line of its kickoff is `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.`, with `<path>` the absolute path of this skill's folder. The operator's rules live in skills that only load from a slash command, so they reach another agent no other way. Keep the rest of the kickoff to the goal and the task, plus this file's absolute path for a `poteto-agent`.
- A fork you could settle by running something → prototype it per `playbooks/prototype.md` instead of asking.
- Code → name the data shape first. Across a function boundary → **architect**. Fan-out → **swarm**. Bakeoff → **arena**. Contested design → **interrogate**.
- Prose, your reply included → **unslop**. Docs, PR descriptions, commit messages → **technical-writing**.
- Before commit → `/deslop` from the `cursor-team-kit` plugin. Before review → **no-comments**.
- Shipping UI / IDE / CLI → `control-ui` or `control-cli` from `cursor-team-kit`.
- A number you measured → **benchmark-checklist**.
- PR status → the **Babysit** playbook, not Cursor's built-in babysit skill. Landing a green stack → the **Shipping** playbook.
- Bot review comments → judge each on its merits.
- Long or unattended work → **show-me-your-work**.
--- patched
- First message → restate the user's goal and the smallest change that meets it, then proceed. Open each subagent brief, PR `## Why`, and final report with the goal.
- Starting any agent → the first line of its `delegate_task` brief is `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.`, with `<path>` the absolute path of this skill's folder. The operator's rules live in skills that only load when the user starts them, so they reach another agent no other way. Keep the rest of the brief to the goal and the task, plus the two `poteto-agent` paths from Subagents for a `poteto-agent`.
- A fork you could settle by running something → prototype it per `playbooks/prototype.md` instead of asking.
- Code → name the data shape first. Across a function boundary → **architect**. Fan-out → **swarm**. Bakeoff → **arena**. Contested design → **interrogate**.
- Prose, your reply included → **unslop**. Docs, PR descriptions, commit messages → **technical-writing**.
- Before commit → **deslop**, if it is installed. Before review → **no-comments**.
- Shipping UI / IDE / CLI → the project's own verification skill if it has one. Otherwise T3's `preview_*` tools for a UI (`preview_snapshot` with `save: true` for a screenshot the user can see), and the shell for a CLI.
- A number you measured → **benchmark-checklist**.
- PR status → the **Babysit** playbook. Landing a green stack → the **Shipping** playbook.
- Bot review comments → judge each on its merits.
- Long or unattended work → **show-me-your-work**.

=== entry 87699a4e00ff
kind: translation
rules: agent-brief, invoke
intent: Playbook-step subagents are briefed to read the poteto-agent prompt file (resolved to an absolute path) and given this skill's path, since T3 has no agent type field. The agent-brief rule says the brief starts with that line, but the trigger above puts the `worker.md` line first in every brief, so the poteto-agent lines follow it. Preserves that the receiving chat runs the playbook itself and that routed skills keep their own briefs. The chat that runs the playbook is named by the skill the user started, with no slash.
--- upstream
**Use `subagent_type: "poteto-agent"` for any subagent you spawn directly inside a playbook step.** The chat that receives `/poteto-mode` runs the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`interrogate`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type`. Respect it.
--- patched
**Brief any subagent you spawn directly inside a playbook step as a `poteto-agent`.** Resolve `../pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` (relative to this skill's folder) and this file to absolute paths. After the `worker.md` line, the brief continues "Read `<abs>/pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` and act as that agent. The poteto-mode skill is at `<abs>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md`." The chat where the user starts the **poteto-mode** skill runs the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`interrogate`, `swarm`) set their own agent briefs. Respect them.

=== entry 88f38a30b2bb
kind: translation
rules: delegate, background, read-only, capabilities, model-role, general-purpose, cursor-only, invoke
intent: Spawn defaults in T3 terms: async `delegate_task` with the whole brief and its own request ID, no `runtimeMode`, and models from the project models file (which the setup-pstack skill writes, named with no slash) or willyskills's role defaults, applied with the model-entry snippet, which also covers `inherit-parent`. Dropped: "Project workers included" (a Cursor Project concept, see the trigger list entry), the `auto` alias, and "spawn `generalPurpose` because `poteto-agent` defaults to Opus 5.5", since a T3 brief carries no model of its own and `inherit-parent` just omits `target`. The `# defaults: 3` migration note is dropped: it detects old Cursor `.mdc` rule files written under Cursor's old default slugs. Preserves which line each kind of work reads, and that per-role lines override the routed skills' choices.
--- upstream
**Defaults for every `Task` call.** `run_in_background: true`, agent mode (readonly strips MCP), file pointers not inlined context, and an explicit model per role, Project workers included. `/setup-pstack` writes `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc`, whose per-role lines override the defaults below and the role models in the routed skills (`arena`, `swarm`, `interrogate`). A role with no line keeps its default. A line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs the role on the parent model, so omit `model` and spawn `generalPurpose`, because `poteto-agent` defaults to Opus 5.5. Each code playbook reads its own line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`, and trivial mechanical edits read `swarm workers`. If the rule has no `# defaults: 3` line, an older setup wrote it. Keep honoring its lines, and tell the user once per session to rerun `/setup-pstack`.
--- patched
**Defaults for every `delegate_task` call.** `mode: "async"`, the inherited runtime mode (never set `runtimeMode`, a narrower mode makes the child wait for approvals no one can give), the whole brief in `task` with file pointers not inlined context, its own `clientRequestId` (reused only to retry that spawn), and an explicit model per role. Call `orchestrator_capabilities` once before the first spawn. Keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. The child's completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of polling, and use `task_status` only for a result needed mid-turn. The **setup-pstack** skill writes {{snippet:models-file}}, whose per-role lines override the defaults below and the role models in the routed skills (`arena`, `swarm`, `interrogate`). A role with no line keeps its default. {{snippet:model-entry}} Each code playbook reads its own line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`, and trivial mechanical edits read `swarm workers`.

=== entry 0753d7b8daac
kind: translation
rules: model-effort, model-role, invoke
intent: The role's effort is a ceiling: keep it for the hardest work, lower it for judgment and routine work, never raise it, and never set fast mode. Preserves that panel seats keep their listed effort, that verification runs on the `swarm workers` line, and that only effort changes, so a diverse panel stays diverse. Drops the Cursor model-catalog rules: Opus 5.5's `-fast` slug, the Gemini seat level, and the rejected-slug fallback chain; `orchestrator_capabilities` lists what each model offers. The setup-pstack skill is named with no slash.
--- upstream
**Match the model to the job.** Start from the role's line or default, keep its family, and set the level the job needs. The `/setup-pstack` budget sets each role's starting level. Panel seats keep their listed level. Raise any other role one step, up to xhigh, for the hardest or subtlest work, lower it one step for judgment, and two or more steps for routine work. No role runs above xhigh. Opus 5.5 always runs its `-fast` slug. Verification always runs on the `swarm workers` model, `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast` by default. A Gemini seat runs at `gemini-4-neon-xhigh` unless its line names another level. If the Task tool rejects a slug, never pass it again. Fall back to the role's line or default, then the calling skill's fallback, then the closest valid slug of the same family, then omit `model`. Say so.
--- patched
**Match the model to the job.** Start from the role's line or default, keep its model, and set the effort the job needs. The **setup-pstack** skill's budget sets each role's starting effort, and that effort is a ceiling. Panel seats keep their listed effort. Keep any other role's effort for the hardest or subtlest work, lower it one step for judgment, and two or more steps for routine work. Never raise it, and never set `fastMode`. Verification always runs on the `swarm workers` line, {{model:swarm workers}} by default. If the model has no effort option at that level, use its nearest level below. `orchestrator_capabilities` lists each model's options.

=== entry 1ad3e0bea581
kind: translation
rules: todolist
intent: The visible step list is `update_plan` in Codex and a list kept in the reply in Claude Code. Preserves that the playbook's steps go in first and the figure-it-out routing.
--- upstream
Match the task to a playbook and open its steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case. Copy its steps into the todolist tool first. Large, cross-cutting, or unattended work, or a task no playbook fits, routes to the **figure-it-out** skill.
--- patched
Match the task to a playbook and open its steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case. Copy its steps into a step list first. Codex keeps it with `update_plan`. Claude Code keeps it in the reply and updates it at each phase. Large, cross-cutting, or unattended work, or a task no playbook fits, routes to the **figure-it-out** skill.

=== entry 9ab9dbbc3978
kind: translation
rules: invoke
intent: The description names both invocations, /poteto-mode for Claude Code and $poteto-mode for Codex. Everything else stays verbatim, including the icon and color keys.
--- upstream
---
name: poteto-mode
description: poteto's agent style for concise, detailed responses, deliberate subagents, unslopped prose, simple code, and verified work. Use for poteto, /poteto-mode, or requests to work in this style.
disable-model-invocation: true
icon: crown
color: yellow
---
--- patched
---
name: poteto-mode
description: poteto's agent style for concise, detailed responses, deliberate subagents, unslopped prose, simple code, and verified work. Use for poteto, /poteto-mode or $poteto-mode, or requests to work in this style.
disable-model-invocation: true
icon: crown
color: yellow
hooks:
  PreToolUse:
    - hooks:
        - type: command
          once: true
          command: mkdir -p "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude" && jq -r .session_id > "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-session"
---

=== entry c1ab5af5119c
kind: translation
rules: model-role
intent: The default model per role is willyskills's default for that role, never a model name. Keeps upstream's grouping (code, prose and judgment, trivial edits, panels) but names each group's own role, since the T3 defaults differ by role. The panel bullet names the two panel roles (`arena runners`, `interrogate reviewers`), whose default lines list one runner per entry.
--- upstream
- Code, prose, and judgment: `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-fast`.
- Trivial mechanical edits: `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast`.
- Diverse review and design panels: one runner each on `claude-fable-5-2-high`, `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast`, and `gemini-4-neon-xhigh`.
--- patched
- Code: {{model:feature, refactoring}} for `feature, refactoring`, {{model:bug-fix}} for `bug-fix`, {{model:perf-issue}} for `perf-issue`, and {{model:hillclimb}} for `hillclimb`.
- Prose and judgment: {{model:judgment and prose}}.
- Trivial mechanical edits: {{model:swarm workers}}.
- Diverse review and design panels: one runner per entry, {{model:arena runners}} for `arena runners` and {{model:interrogate reviewers}} for `interrogate reviewers`.

=== entry ce7bd647a16c
kind: translation
rules: model-role
intent: Deletes the block. It makes one Cursor catalog model (Fable 5.2) an exception that runs outside a panel only when named or after two failed runs of another named model. Here every spawn takes its role's line, so a model the user wants runs by putting it in that line.
--- upstream
**Fable 5.2 is an exception.** It runs outside a panel only when the user named it for this task or two Opus 5.5 xhigh runs of the same brief failed its check. Say which. Difficulty alone never qualifies.
--- patched
